  dek:"This is just evaporation, and the only way to ruin it is impatience. Above a bare simmer the milk sugars catch on the base and the whole batch tastes scorched. Forty-five minutes at low heat with the occasional stir gets you there; twenty minutes at medium gets you something brown and bitter.",
  card:"2 cups milk + ¾ cup sugar, reduced by half at a bare simmer. 45 min, no shortcuts.",
  ing:[
    {n:"Whole milk", q:"Two cups. Skim will not work — the fat matters.", amt:"2 cups", shelf:"milk"},
    {n:"Sugar", q:"Three quarters of a cup.", amt:"¾ cup", shelf:"unrefined-sugar"},
    {n:"Butter", q:"A tablespoon, optional, for a richer result.", amt:"1 tbsp", shelf:"butter"},
    {n:"Baking soda", q:"A pinch, optional — keeps it paler by slowing browning.", amt:"pinch", local:true},
  ],
  steps:[
    {b:"Dissolve the sugar first", t:"Milk and sugar over low heat, stirred until completely dissolved before it gets anywhere near a simmer."},
    {b:"Bare simmer, 45 minutes", t:"The occasional lazy bubble. Stir every five minutes, scraping the base. Higher heat scorches and there is no recovering it."},
    {b:"Stop at half volume", t:"About a cup and a half left, and thick enough to coat a spoon. It thickens considerably more as it cools."},
    {b:"Cool and jar", t:"Two weeks refrigerated."},
    {b:"For dulce de leche", t:"Keep going another thirty minutes until it darkens to caramel, stirring constantly."},
    {b:"Make it yours", t:"Coconut milk makes a dairy-free version that works in Vietnamese coffee. Vanilla or a cinnamon stick infused in. It goes into key lime pie, tres leches, and iced coffee."},
  ],
